  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current algorithm uses a multi-stage optimization approach combining SLSQP, simulated annealing, and fine-grained search, but it can get stuck in local optima. This alternative approach uses a hybrid method combining gradient information with parameterized periodic structures to improve global exploration while maintaining local refinement.\n\nThe strategy leverages the insight that optimal solutions often exhibit periodicity and symmetry. We generate candidate solutions by:\n1. Starting from a periodic basis with minimal amplitude\n2. Using gradient-based adjustment to smooth the function\n3. Incorporating explicit symmetry constraints\n4. Performing region-adaptive optimization to focus on worst-lag areas\n5. Using adaptive sampling of solution space\n\nA novel component is the parameterization of harmonic content in the function, allowing us to manipulate the overlap characteristics directly. This reduces the dependency on local minima by changing the underlying structure rather than merely adjusting values.\n\nMathematical specification\nWe represent the solution as a piecewise constant function h(x) with values in [0,1] satisfying \u222bh(x)dx=1. The objective is min C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx.\n\nWe parameterize h(x) using Fourier series coefficients, which allows us to:\n- Control periodicity\n- Enforce symmetry constraints\n- Compute derivatives for gradient-based optimization\n\nThe Fourier representation is truncated to maintain feasibility (0\u2264h(x)\u22641) and we use a dictionary of basis functions to ensure optimality conditions are met.\n\nThe algorithm:\n1. Initializes with harmonic parameterization\n2. Uses constrained optimization with explicit symmetry\n3. Projects solutions back to the feasible space\n4. Adjusts frequencies based on overlap gradient\n5. Combines modes from different periodic bases\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Setup\n   - Input: n (number of samples, 280 for initial run)\n   - Initialize h to uniform distribution (n/2)\n   - dx = 2.0/n\n\n2. Parameterization\n   - Define Fourier basis functions {cos(2\u03c0kx/D), sin(2\u03c0kx/D)} for k=0 to K_max\n   - Truncate and normalize to satisfy \u222bh(x)dx=1\n   - Add explicit symmetry enforcement terms\n\n3. Optimization loop\n   for i in range(max_iter):\n      a. Compute overlap at k=-(n-1) to k=n-1\n      b. Combine with gradient using exponential smoothing\n      c. Solve for coefficients with convex constraints\n      d. Project solution to [0,1] domain\n      e. Update frequency parameters based on gradient magnitude\n\n4. Frequencies adjustment\n   - Estimate local minima in overlap\n   - Shift frequency parameters towards regions of high correlation\n   - Maintain global structure while refining details\n\n5. Final scoring\n   - Re-evaluate original objective\n   - Check feasibility\n   - Project final solution to ensure integral constraint\n\nSearch choices and variations\n1. Harmony basis: Use either cosine or sine weighted basis depending on symmetry needs\n2. Frequency range: Automatically determined from correlation peaks\n3. Modes of operation:\n   - Harmonic surgery: Target specific lags for improvement\n   - Symmetry enforcement: Toggle based on lag symmetry detection\n   - Adaptivity: Adjust amplitudes based on time remaining\n\nWe start with base frequencies detected from correlation peaks and iteratively refine using gradient information.\n\nCompute budget and robustness\n- Primary loop: 500 iterations, each requiring an overlap calculation\n- Early termination for peaks below 0.38\n- Repair function to 280 points if upsampled\n- Intelligent dimension reduction when necessary\n\nThe algorithm carefully monitors:\n- Convergence rate via gradient norm\n- Feature correlation at critical lags\n- Time consumed per iteration\n\nValidation returns a solution with:\n   0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   sum(h) = n/2 (maintained by projection)\n   original objective computed via correlate(...)\n\nThe method ensures that all candidates are evaluated with the exact Erd\u0151s scoring function and includes a memory of the best solution found so far.",
